Phase 1: Deterministic Extraction Engine (serves R5, R6, R7)

### What it does

A pure JavaScript function that takes frames' HTML+CSS as input and produces three structured
outputs. No AI involvement. Same input → identical output, every time.

### 1.1 — New file: netlify/functions/design-extractor.mjs

A standalone module, imported by mcp.mjs. Contains:

Function: extractDesignSystem(frames)

Input: [{ name, html, css, width, height, layoutMode }]
Output: structured object (see below)

The extraction algorithm (each step is deterministic):

```
  Step 1: CSS Parsing
   - Concatenate all frame CSS into one string
   - Regex parse into rules: { selector, property, value }
   - Handle @import (extract font URLs), @keyframes (store raw), @media (store raw)

  Step 2: Color Extraction
   - Walk all rules where property ∈ [background, background-color, color, border, border-color,
border-top, fill, box-shadow]
   - Parse each value: extract hex (#xxx, #xxxxxx), rgb(), rgba(), named colors
   - Count occurrences across all frames
   - Cluster by frequency
   - Classify by role:
   - Most frequent background → --color-canvas
   - Most frequent text color → --color-text
   - Highest saturation (HSL S > 0.7) → --color-accent
   - Low saturation, medium lightness → --color-muted
   - Near-white → --color-surface
   - Near-black → --color-ink
   - Output: [{ name, value, hex, role, usageCount }]

  Step 3: Typography Extraction
   - Walk all rules where property ∈ [font-family, font-size, font-weight, line-height,
letter-spacing]
   - Group font-family values, rank by frequency → primary, secondary, fallback
   - Collect all font-size values, sort ascending → type scale
   - Collect all font-weight values → weight range
   - Collect all line-height values → leading scale
   - Collect all letter-spacing values → tracking scale
   - Output: { families: [], typeScale: [], weights: [], leading: [], tracking: [] }

  Step 4: Spacing Extraction
   - Walk all rules where property ∈ [padding, margin, gap, padding-top, margin-bottom, ...]
   - Extract px values, cluster into a scale
   - Output: [4px, 8px, 10px, 15px, 20px, ...]

  Step 5: Border Radius Extraction
   - Collect all border-radius values
   - Output: [5px, 8px, 10px, 14px, 50%]

  Step 6: Shadow Extraction
   - Collect all box-shadow values verbatim
   - Output: [{ value, usageCount }]

  Step 7: Component Detection
   - Parse HTML of each frame with cheerio
   - Extract all class combinations per element: [{ tag, classes, depth }]
   - Find class groups that appear in 3+ frames with same tag → registered component
   - Example: div.cta appears in all 10 frames → Component: "CTA Button"
   - Output: [{ name, selector, tag, classCombo, frameCount, sampleHtml }]

  Step 8: Constraint Detection (facts, not advice)
   - Count unique saturated colors → "Palette uses N accent color(s)"
   - Check if any body text uses accent color → "Warning: accent used for body text"
   - Count fonts → "Typography uses N families"
   - Check for @keyframes → "N animations detected"
   - Output: [{ type: "fact", statement: "..." }]
```

